    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Calculus and numerical methods\"\ncourse-unit: p03-calculus-and-numerical-methods\nstatus: complete\n---\n\n## Why this unit matters\n\n- Differential equations change instant-to-instant, but a computer takes\n  **finite steps**.\n- We review the derivative as a rate, build the **forward Euler** integrator,\n  and analyse its error — local vs global.\n- These ideas justify the adaptive solvers used from the ODE chapter onward.\n\n## Equations {.smaller}\n\nA first-order Taylor expansion,\n\n$$\nu(t+\\delta t)=u(t)+\\delta t\\,u'(t)+\\tfrac12\\delta t^2 u''(\\xi),\n$$ {#eq-s-p03-taylor}\n\ndropping the remainder gives the **forward Euler** update\n\n$$\nu_{n+1}=u_n+\\delta t\\,f(u_n).\n$$ {#eq-s-p03-euler}\n\nLocal truncation error is $O(\\delta t^2)$; over $T/\\delta t$ steps the **global**\nerror is one order lower, $O(\\delta t)$ — slope 1 on log–log axes.\n\n## Julia API: the integrator\n\n::: {#cell-s-p03-kernel .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\nfunction sir_ode!(du, u, p, t)\n    S, I, R = u; β, c, γ, N = p\n    λ = β * c * I / N\n    du[1] = -λ * S; du[2] = λ * S - γ * I; du[3] = γ * I; return nothing\nend\n\nfunction euler(f, u0, p, δt, tmax)\n    ts = 0.0:δt:tmax\n    us = Matrix{Float64}(undef, length(u0), length(ts)); us[:, 1] .= u0\n    du = similar(u0)\n    for n in 1:length(ts)-1\n        f(du, @view(us[:, n]), p, ts[n])\n        us[:, n+1] .= us[:, n] .+ δt .* du     # u_{n+1} = u_n + δt f(u_n)\n    end\n    collect(ts), us\nend\n\nu0 = [990.0, 10.0, 0.0]; p = (0.05, 10.0, 0.25, 1000.0); tmax = 40.0\n_, us = euler(sir_ode!, u0, p, 0.1, tmax); round.(us[:, end]; digits = 2)\n```\n\n::: {#s-p03-kernel .cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 208.19\n  14.33\n 777.48\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n## Generated result: convergence\n\n::: {#cell-fig-s-p03-convergence .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\nusing CairoMakie\nδt_ref = tmax / 2^16; _, us_ref = euler(sir_ode!, u0, p, δt_ref, tmax); u_ref = us_ref[:, end]\nδts = [tmax / 2^k for k in 6:11]\nerrs = map(δts) do δt\n    _, u = euler(sir_ode!, u0, p, δt, tmax); maximum(abs.(u[:, end] .- u_ref))\nend\nfig = Figure(size = (720, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1,1]; xscale = log10, yscale = log10,\n          xlabel = \"δt\", ylabel = \"global error at t = 40\")\nscatter!(ax, δts, errs; markersize = 12, color = :firebrick, label = \"measured\")\nlines!(ax, δts, errs[1] .* (δts ./ δts[1]); linestyle = :dash, color = :black, label = \"slope 1\")\naxislegend(ax; position = :rb); fig\n```\n\n::: {#fig-s-p03-convergence .cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```{=html}\n<img width=720 height=400 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>\n```\n\nGlobal error of forward Euler at $t=40$ vs step size. Measured points parallel the slope-1 reference, confirming $O(\\delta t)$ convergence.\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n## Validation\n\nEmpirical order (successive halvings) should be ≈ 1, and Euler preserves the\nconservation law at any step:\n\n::: {#cell-s-p03-check .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\norders = [log2(errs[k] / errs[k+1]) for k in 1:length(errs)-1]\nmean_order = sum(orders) / length(orders)\n_, uc = euler(sir_ode!, u0, p, 0.5, tmax)\ncons_ok = all(t -> isapprox(t, 1000.0; atol = 1e-9), vec(sum(uc; dims = 1)))\n(round(mean_order; digits = 3), isapprox(mean_order, 1.0; atol = 0.1), cons_ok)\n```\n\n::: {#s-p03-check .cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(1.019, true, true)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n## Limitations & takeaways\n\n- Accuracy improves only **linearly** — halving the error doubles the cost.\n- Forward Euler is only **conditionally stable**: too-large steps oscillate or\n  diverge on stiff problems.\n- Adaptive higher-order methods (`Tsit5`, …) get far more accuracy per unit work.\n- **Takeaway:** understand order and stability, then let SciML choose the step.\n\n::: {.notes}\nDraw the distinction between local truncation error and accumulated global error\nexplicitly; the one-order drop is the crux. Note the reference solution is a very\nfine Euler run, so we measure convergence to Euler's own limit. Mention that this\nmotivates adopting Tsit5 and adaptive stepping from the ODE chapter, and invite\nlearners to try the midpoint method in the notebook to see order 2.\n:::\n\n## Learn more\n\n- Book chapter: [Calculus and numerical methods](../../prerequisites/03-calculus-and-numerical-methods.html)\n- Pluto notebook: [`03-calculus-and-numerical-methods.jl`](../../notebooks/prerequisites/03-calculus-and-numerical-methods.jl)\n- [OrdinaryDiffEq solvers](https://docs.sciml.ai/OrdinaryDiffEq/stable/)\n\n",
